ing in utero and lactational exposure to genistein at levels comparable to human dietary exposure</article-title><source>Toxicol Lett</source><year>2002</year><volume>133</volume><fpage>181</fpage><lpage>191</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12119126</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B29"><label>29</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Hudgins</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Schorr</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Sommer</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Peterson</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Flaws</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Furth</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>In utero and lactational treatment with 2,3,7,8-tetrachlorodibenzo-p-dioxin impairs mammary gland differentiation but does not block the response to exogenous estrogen in the postpubertal female rat</article-title><source>Toxicol Sci</source><year>2001</year><volume>62</volume><fpage>46</fpage><lpage>53</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11399792</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Percent of rats with CIS or INV when treated with different doses of DMBA at various ages either 21, 50 or 100 days. Plotted is the percent of rats with either CIS or INV for each group, determined 3 months post-DMBA exposure. For all ages, n=5 rats (corn oil) and n=20 for each DMBA treated group.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijbsv03p0408g01"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>(a) Percent of rats with incidence of various pathologies when treated with 0 (corn oil), 20, 50 and 100 mg/kg BW DMBA at 21 days of age; (b) at 50 days of age; (c) at 100 days Plotted is the percent of rats with each pathology for each treatment group, determined 3 months post-DMBA exposure for n=5 rats (corn oil) and n=20 for each DMBA treated group. (d) is the number of pathologies per rat when treated with 0 (corn oil), 20, 50 and 100 mg/kg BW DMBA at 21 days of age; (e) at 50 days, and (f) at 100 days. Plotted is the mean + s.d. number of each pathology per rat for each group, determined 3 months post-DMBA exposure for n=5 rats (corn oil) and n=20 for each DMBA treated group.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijbsv03p0408g02"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Panels (a) (b) and (c) are representative samples of histological sections of mammary glands from virgin corn oil treated rat at time of sacrifice (approx. 190 days old). The tissue sections are stained with HE and all are x 20 magnification. The arrows in panel (a) indicate milk-filled cystic spaces; in panel (b) the * indicates specific lobules which are massively expanded by milk; panel (c) shows milk filled ducts.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijbsv03p0408g03"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Panels (a) and (b) show histological sections of pituitary glands confirmed by hematoxalin and eosin staining (not shown) from 100 day old virgin rats dosed with vehicle then sacrificed at approximately 190 days of age. The tissue sections are stained for prolactin and both are at a 10X magnification. Panel (a) is taken from a Charles River Sprague-Dawley rat while panel (b) is from a Taconic Sprague-Dawley rat.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijbsv03p0408g04"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Panels (a), (b), and (c) show mammary morphology from animals at (a) 21 days, (b) 50 days, and (c) 100 days.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijbsv03p0408g05"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Calculated IC50 for Charles River SD</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead valign="top"><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age at Initiation (days)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IC50 (mg/kg)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">95% CI</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">21</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">21.86</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">18.80-25.41</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.03</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.65-16.85</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.11</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.22-10.59</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Tumor Distribution by Type in Percent (and Total Count)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead valign="top"><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">DMBA (mg/kg)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Benign</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mixed</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CIS</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">INV</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A</td><td colspan="4" align="center" rowspan="1">21 Days</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0(oil)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0 (0/0)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0 (0/0)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0 (0/0)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0 (0/0)</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0 (0/35)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (3/35)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">91 (32/35)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0 (0/35)</td></tr><tr><td